userStories.description:
**Goal** — Route enabled escape requests through human approval except for allowed raw calls, retaining policy decisions and original tool input.

**Motivation** — `escalate` mode only turns a grant miss into an ask; nothing lets one command run unwrapped.

**Approach** — With the option enabled and the flag set, an `ask` verdict proceeds with `unsandboxed: true` and the suffix; an allow verdict under `gated` or `escalate` (Bash) or an ask-less `Exec` grant becomes an ask with rule `Unsandboxed(<identity>)`; an allow under `raw` runs without asking.

With no launcher (dropped by `allowUnsandboxed`) the routing is the same and the tool direct-spawns on allow.

The runtime's `callTool` reads the field first, but only for calls whose policy identity is `Bash` or `Exec` (a RunCommand declared-command call is left to the tool, which rejects the flag itself):
- option off and `input.unsandboxed === true` -> outcome `{ kind: "error", content: "unsandboxed commands are not enabled for this session (allowUnsandboxedCommands)" }`, recorded through `logCall` like the unknown-tool refusal, before any policy evaluation or ask, nothing spawned;
- otherwise the policy verdict is computed exactly as without the flag;
- a deny stays a deny (same `reason`, same `breach`);
- an `ask` verdict proceeds as an ask whose `AskRequest` carries `unsandboxed: true` and a reason suffix;
- an allow verdict under any Bash approval mode other than `raw` is converted to an ask whose `rule` is `Unsandboxed(<identity>)` and whose `matchedRule` stays unset (no ask rule matched, so remembered approvals derive no origin from it), so `gated`, `escalate`, and `Exec` calls that have no ask rule all reach the human;
- an allow verdict under `raw` runs.
On allow the tool passes `unwrapped: true` to the launcher. A session whose launcher was dropped by `allowUnsandboxed` has nothing to unwrap: the tool direct-spawns as it does for every call, and the ask still says the command runs outside the sandbox, which is true.

`resolveUnsandboxedCall(args)` — Otherwise returns a marker that makes `callTool` (a) evaluate the policy with the field ignored, (b) convert an allow verdict into an ask with `rule: "Unsandboxed(<identity>)"` and reason `unsandboxed run requested` when `bashApproval !== "raw"`, and (c) pass `{ suffix: unsandboxedReasonSuffix(input) }` into `resolveAskOutcome` for any ask it raises.

Why no approval event under `raw`: `raw` never asks, by definition (ADR-030), and the user's "full" mode never asks. A session today records tool calls through a non-recording sink (`buildSessionToolSupport` passes no `auditDir`, so `supportSink` returns `createNoOpToolAuditSink()`), so there is no durable session ledger to assert on; the sandbox record with `reason: "requested"` reaches the audit row wherever a caller does configure `auditDir`, and the host observes the unwrapped run through the launcher. Plumbing an audit directory into sessions is out of scope here.

**Scope** — In: complete the helper/marker and runtime policy-ask routing introduced by US-005, preserve resolvedPaths on converted verdicts, decorate real asks using US-004, run original input on allow, preserve denied outcomes and no execution when the human rejects a converted ask, and extend US-005's runtime-unsandboxed.test.ts. Out: redesigning US-005 enablement/refusal, session model-driven cases (US-007), RunCommand tool implementation (US-008), new ask kinds, durable session auditing or approval caches. The guard-denial ask path (`commandShadow.guard` in `callTool`) does not carry the `unsandboxed` flag; sessions configure no command shadow, so it is inert here. Launcher-level relaxations that read the session's sandbox state (`rawScreenOptionsFor`, `isTempConfined`) are not re-decided per call; they are inert in sessions and unchanged for nax run callers. Keep callTool under its limit by putting routing in the single deep-module helper, not inline branches.

**Interface** — Existing runtime option: `unsandboxed?: { enabled: boolean; bashApproval: BashApprovalMode }`. `resolveAskOutcome` metadata: `unsandboxed?: { readonly suffix: string }`. Converted verdict display rule: `"Unsandboxed(<identity>)"`; reason: `"unsandboxed run requested"`; matchedRule stays unset. The helper and reason renderer are not new package entry exports.
